# The kernel's term language, the syntax of every expression the type checker reasons about. The checker is
# bidirectional and works by normalization: it evaluates a term to a normal form and compares normal forms. This file
# declares the shape of every term constructor. The typing rules that relate them live alongside each construct (the
# function, pair, identity, self, universe, and multiplicity files); here is the bare grammar, faithful to the kernel.
# Each construct is a form whose fields are its subterms. A term is one of these forms.

# A term: any expression in the kernel. The shape is the specific constructor, one of the forms below.
form term
  link shape, note private

# A variable, a reference to an enclosing binding, addressed by how many binders out it sits (a de Bruijn index).
form variable
  link index, like natural

# A metavariable, an inference hole the checker fills by unification while elaborating a program.
form hole
  link mark, like natural

# A postulated constant: a base type or primitive named in the signature, with no definition to unfold.
form constant
  link name, like text

# A universe, the type of types at a given level. See the universe file for the hierarchy.
form sort
  link level, like natural

# A lambda, the introduction of a function type: a body living under one new binding.
form lambda
  link body, like term

# An application, the elimination of a function type: a function applied to an argument.
form application
  link target, like term
  link input, like term

# An annotation, a term carried with its stated type, the seam where checking meets inference.
form annotation
  link subject, like term
  link signature, like term

# Reflexivity, the canonical proof that a value equals itself, inhabiting its identity type.
form reflection
  link space, like term
  link value, like term

# The identity eliminator (J): given a proof that two values are equal and a motive that holds at reflexivity, the
# property holds along the whole proof. This is how every fact about equality is derived.
form journey
  link proof, like term
  link motive, like term
  link base, like term

# A pair, the introduction of a dependent pair type: a first component and a second that may depend on it.
form couple
  link first, like term
  link second, like term

# The first projection of a pair.
form front
  link couple, like term

# The second projection of a pair.
form back
  link couple, like term
